No wait, I discussed this with an LLM and it had a great suggestions - Image.open() in PIL natively supports an IOstream or a bytes object. And urllib.request.urlopen creates an HTTP response object whose .read method returns the raw bytes payload - this is what you use when you do
with urllib.request.urlopen(url) as file:
	file.read()

anyways, there's also io.BytesIO() which wraps the raw bytes returned from urllib into an in-memory binary stream which can be processed by Image.open (if you don't use it it causes valueerror: embedded null byte). This was a great suggestion as the previous one was touching the userspace. So I implemented it: sciagram $TEST_URL --color --method=luminosity works perfectly. test url is just export TEST_URL="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/John_Carmack_2025.jpg". Note that I am using a naive check for the weburl (if the image path contains "http://" or "https://") - this will have to be OPTIMISED later.

